\details{
\strong{Numeric traits.} Two records match when they are for the same
\code{taxon_name} and \code{trait_name} and their values agree once both are rounded to
the precision of the less precise one -- so \code{1.23} matches \code{1.2}, but \code{1.26}
does not. Precision is the number of significant figures in the value as
stored, capped at 6. When either value has fewer than \code{min_sig_figs}
significant figures, the values must be exactly equal, since \code{2} would
otherwise match anything from \code{1.5} to \code{2.49}. Bins (\code{value_type} \code{bin}, e.g.
\code{1--5}) must be identical. Records that both have coordinates must also be
within \code{max_distance_km} of each other; records without coordinates are
matched on value alone, as most species-level data (floras, compilations)
have none.

\strong{Categorical traits.} A set value per species is the norm, so identical
values are only compared when both records have coordinates within
\code{max_distance_km}, and a pair of datasets is only reported when such matches
span at least \code{min_categorical_taxa} taxa. Traits of type \code{month_presence}
and \code{text} are compared in the same way.

\strong{Telling duplicates from coincidences.} Genuinely duplicated data usually
shows up as a high \code{prop_matched_combos} -- nearly every taxon x trait the two
datasets share has a matching value -- across several taxa, often with
\code{n_obs_pairs_multi_trait} > 0 (the same pair of observations matching on
several traits) and a low \code{p_chance}. Beware traits with a narrow range that
are commonly reported to two significant figures, such as leaf nitrogen
concentration: \code{p_chance} is high for these, and a large pair of datasets
will share some values by chance.
